  \"common_name\": \"lactonitrile, stereochemistry unspecified\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H5NO/c1-3(5)2-4/h3,5H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_030\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactic acid, stereochemistry unspecified\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2,4H,1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-fed reducing discharge supplying cyanide and carbonyl compounds\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 293-330 K in the receiving water; the electrical discharge is nonthermal\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar in a circulating laboratory analogue\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic CH4/NH3/H2/water-vapor gas circulation with repeated electrical discharge and aqueous capture over approximately one week. Proposed field analogue: a transient reducing volcanic plume over a cool receiving pool, not a globally methane/ammonia-dominated atmosphere. Rapid condensation, dissolution, and subsequent chemical trapping are required; a partly enclosed pore or basin limits volatile losses. H2 is co-fed and returned, with possible net production.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lumped radical/plasma source reaction, not an elementary mechanism. One balanced bookkeeping example is 4 CH4 + NH3 + 2 H2O + H2 -> CH3CHO + HCHO + HCN + 9 H2. Parallel radical reactions generate cyanide and carbonyl compounds before aqueous Strecker chemistry. The coefficients do not specify experimental product ratios, and numerous untracked minor products also form.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller (1955, 1957) supports discharge production of aldehydes and HCN followed by aminonitrile chemistry; the resulting alanine was DL. Johnson et al. (2008) supports a volcanic-discharge analogue. Trail et al. (2011) argues against assuming a persistently strongly reducing global atmosphere, so this node requires a local or episodic reduced-gas source. No iron catalyst is assigned because Miller (1957) did not find a material advantage from added ferrous salt. Capture efficiency and natural production flux remain unquantified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Nitrogen-fed reducing discharge with energetic nitrogen activation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 293-330 K in condensate; nonthermal discharge in the gas phase\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar in the experimental analogue\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic CH4/N2/H2/water-vapor mixture with repeated energetic discharge and cool aqueous capture. Nitrogen fixation is driven by discharge energy, not by thermal equilibrium at the condensate temperature. Use the same short-transfer, low-headspace capture concept as rxn_001. The experiment's lower yield must not be replaced by the ammonia-fed source yield.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lumped nitrogen-activation and carbonyl-source node. An illustrative balanced equation is 4 CH4 + N2 + 2 H2O + H2 -> CH3CHO + HCHO + HCN + NH3 + 6 H2. Discharge activation of N2 enables formation of cyanide and reduced nitrogen, while methane/water chemistry supplies carbonyl compounds. This equation is an elemental accounting example, not a measured distribution or a resolved sequence of intermediates.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller (1957) obtained the same general amino-acid product family when N2 replaced initially supplied NH3, although at lower yield. This establishes energetic nitrogen entry, but the supplied report does not quantify the individual ammonia and aldehyde source fluxes represented here. Their partitioning is therefore an incompletely characterized lumped-source model. The route does not assume that ordinary UV alone performs the same chemistry, and it retains the reduced-carbon and volatile-capture limitations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"NiS-mediated acetylene hydration supplying acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Freshly precipitated nickel sulfide, NiS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"378 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Confined aqueous reactor above water's vapor pressure at 378 K; acetylene and CO gas activities maintained\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"NiS\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acetylene and CO contact fresh NiS in a sealed, shallow hydrothermal pore or reactor. The supplied report does not establish natural gas partial pressures. CO is retained as an experimentally relevant feed/conditioning gas but is not assigned net consumption in the hydration subreaction. Downstream use requires cooling and aqueous transfer through confined fractures, or reagent addition before substantial acetaldehyde escape.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced hydration subreaction: C2H2 + H2O + CO -> CH3CHO + CO. NiS promotes hydration of acetylene, with enol-to-aldehyde conversion furnishing acetaldehyde. CO appears on both sides because its presence in the tested reaction matrix is retained without inventing carbon incorporation into this isolated C2 transformation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The acetylene/NiS study in Communications Chemistry (2023; author names are not given in the supplied report) demonstrated millimolar acetaldehyde at 105 degrees C; catalyst-free controls gave less than approximately 40 micromolar, and FeS alone did not provide detectable free acetaldehyde. Subsequent cyanide/ammonium addition gave only approximately 180 micromolar alanine after seven days. Thus acetaldehyde formation is supported, but gas supply, volatile retention, pH sequencing, and overall alanine efficiency remain limiting.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetaldehyde Strecker conversion to alaninonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"303-333 K as a proposed concentrated-pool operating window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Buffered, anoxic water around pH 8.5-10 with sufficient NH3/NH4+ relative to carbonyl and cyanide. Use short aqueous transfer from discharge condensate, or cooled NiS-pore effluent. Aldehyde and HCN should be chemically trapped before extensive open-pool evaporation. The exact natural residence time and conversion at dilute concentrations are not established.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced net reaction: CH3CHO + NH3 + HCN -> CH3CH(NH2)CN + H2O. Ammonia addition gives an aminoalcohol, dehydration gives an imine/iminium species, and cyanide addition forms alaninonitrile. Achiral reagents do not specify the configuration at the newly formed stereocenter.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller (1957) supports the aldehyde/HCN/aminonitrile mechanism. The Communications Chemistry acetylene/NiS study (2023) detected 1-aminoethan-1-ol and 2-aminopropanenitrile after restoring alkaline conditions, providing direct intermediate evidence. The proposed cooler surface window is an environmental implementation rather than a quoted optimum from that hydrothermal study. Low ammonia activity or acidic conditions divert carbon toward lactonitrile, represented by rxn_016.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Alaninonitrile hydrolysis producing both alanine enantiomers\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Br\u00f8nsted acid, regenerated during hydrolysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K, proposed warm hydrolysis pocket\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"H+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Aqueous, UV-shielded acidic geothermal pocket, provisionally pH 1-3, reached after aminonitrile formation rather than during cyanide capture. Hydrolysis proceeds over days or longer depending on acidity and temperature. This is not a claim that mild natural water reproduces rapid laboratory strong-acid hydrolysis. Product removal to cooler water limits prolonged thermal exposure.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced racemic-reference equation: 2 CH3CH(NH2)CN + 4 H2O -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 NH3. Nitrile hydration proceeds through an amide and then carboxylic acid formation. The two product nodes describe molecules in the same unresolved solution, not a separation operation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller (1955) identified DL-alanine, and Miller (1957) showed that aminonitrile hydrolysis could account for amino-acid accumulation on approximately week-long experimental timescales. The specific natural pH/temperature window proposed here requires kinetic validation and is not presented as a measured optimum. Equal enantiomer production is appropriate for an achiral Strecker source; no L-selective process or isolated L product is inferred.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Ni3Fe-catalyzed CO2 hydrogenation supplying dilute pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni3Fe alloy particles, an awaruite-like catalytic material\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"298 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"25 bar total CO2/H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni3Fe\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Experimental benchmark: approximately pH 6, CO2:H2 gas ratio 3:2, 24 hours. Physical setting: cool, pressurized, serpentinization-fed mineral pores, not a 350-600 K vent interior and not an enzyme-containing system. Downstream fluid transfer requires retained pyruvate, a change in gas activity and pH, and suitable residence time. Neither selective pyruvate concentration nor coupled alanine synthesis was demonstrated in this experiment.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The pyruvate branch has the balanced net equation 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> C3H4O3 + 3 H2O. Parallel formate and acetate formation are CO2 + H2 -> CH2O2 and 2 CO2 + 4 H2 -> C2H4O2 + 2 H2O. Their illustrative sum is 6 CO2 + 10 H2 -> C3H4O3 + CH2O2 + C2H4O2 + 5 H2O. This sum does not imply equal product fluxes or a single elementary catalytic cycle.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Beyazay et al. (2023) measured approximately 26.72 mM formate, 0.04 mM acetate, and 0.02 mM pyruvate under this benchmark. These observations justify the carbon-source connection but identify pyruvate as a minor product. Kelley et al. (2005) supports H2-rich serpentinizing fluids as a resource analogue, not these exact reactor conditions. The roughly 1500-fold concentration difference from the Kaur et al. (2024) amination benchmark is an unresolved integration problem, not proof that dilute conversion is impossible.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Fresh ferrous-hydroxide-promoted pyruvate reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_018\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fresh Fe(OH)2 as a consumable reducing solid, not a trace recyclable catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-348 K, within the reported warm aqueous experimental series\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, UV-shielded alkaline spring sediment or confined mineral slurry near pH 9. Fresh precipitate, substantial mineral excess, excess NH3/NH4+, and hours-to-day contact are required experimental features. The supplied report does not give a separate optimized Fe(OH)2-only recipe. Transfer from rxn_006 requires pH adjustment by anoxic fluid mixing; conversion of its approximately 0.02 mM pyruvate effluent has not been measured.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formal Fe(II)-to-Fe(III) balance: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 4 Fe(OH)2 -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 H2O + 4 FeOOH. Pyruvate/ammonia condensation is followed by reduction of the C=N functionality. FeOOH denotes oxidation-state and hydration-equivalent accounting for a hydrous ferric product, not identification of a particular mineral phase.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003) demonstrated reductive amination with freshly precipitated FeS or Fe(OH)2. Their approximately 52 mol% alanine maximum was reported for the FeS system and is not transferred to this Fe(OH)2 node. Carbon-to-alanine conversion has experimental support; the exact spent-solid composition is not established in the supplied evidence, so the FeOOH product representation is explicitly provisional bookkeeping. No unsupported pyrite coproduct or catalytic regeneration cycle is asserted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Mixed-valence iron-precipitate-promoted pyruvate reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fresh mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxide-oxyhydroxide precipitate with approximately 1:1 initial Fe(II):Fe(III)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Mixed-valence iron precipitate; its Fe(II) reducing inventory is consumable\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Experimental benchmark: pH 10, approximately 1:1 Fe(II):Fe(III), and 48-72 hours in closed batch-like conditions with excess ammonia. Field analogue: a long-residence, anoxic, mineral-rich spring sediment pore rather than a rapidly flowing chimney. Initial ferric material is an external mineral resource; its local production or delivery under anoxia must be established independently.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A balanced bookkeeping example retaining an initial ferric inventory is 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 4 Fe(OH)2 + 4 FeOOH -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 H2O + 8 FeOOH. Mixed-valence surfaces influence condensation and reduction selectivity. The FeOOH formula represents hydrous ferric equivalents, not an experimentally resolved transformation into a specific crystalline phase.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Barge et al. (2019) found approximately 70% alanine and 30% lactate among analyzed liquid organic products under favorable warm, alkaline batch conditions. Fully ferric material was inactive, while strongly ferrous material favored lactate. Their chimney experiments did not detect alanine, and ammonia excess was acknowledged as a limitation. Accordingly, neither the batch product fraction nor the provisional solid-phase balance is treated as a demonstrated flowing-environment yield. Anoxic availability of the initial mixed-valence material and coupling to dilute upstream pyruvate remain conditional.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Nickel-catalyzed H2-driven pyruvate reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Accessible metallic nickel surface\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Experimental benchmark: pH 7-8, 30 mM pyruvate, 6-150 mM ammonium, 24 hours. Physical setting: cool, confined, anoxic mineral pores receiving H2-rich water; no enzymes or membranes are required. Transfer from the CO2-fixation zone must change CO2/H2 activity and pH without losing pyruvate. Sustained gas pressure and 24-hour residence are boundary conditions, not assumptions about an open surface pool.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced racemic-reference equation: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 2 H2 -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 H2O. Nickel activates H2 and promotes hydrogenation of a pyruvate-derived imine/iminium intermediate. Hydrogen is the external electron donor; the metal is catalytic rather than the stoichiometric reductant.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) demonstrated this chemistry and reported up to approximately 17% alanine with Ni at 6 mM ammonia and 30 mM pyruvate, with that yield referenced to ammonia. Nickel is chosen over Pd on geochemical abundance grounds despite Pd's higher laboratory yield. The reported yield is not applied to the approximately 0.02 mM pyruvate from Beyazay et al. (2023). Lactate formation competes and is explicitly represented by rxn_018; an achiral surface gives no established L preference.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Meteoritic Fe-Ni-catalyzed H2-driven pyruvate reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Exposed Fe-Ni iron-meteorite material, represented by the Aletai experimental analogue\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2 in the demonstrated reaction family\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Meteoritic Fe-Ni alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Near-neutral anoxic water, approximately pH 7-8, ammonium present, and approximately 24-hour contact. Physical setting: confined water contacting impact-fragmented or otherwise exposed meteoritic metal. Laboratory powders have much greater accessible area than intact meteorites; catalyst area and passivation must be measured before scaling. The dilute upstream pyruvate integration gap is unchanged from rxn_009.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced racemic-reference equation: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 2 H2 -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 H2O. Ni-containing alloy sites catalyze H2 activation and imine reduction. No net Fe or Ni consumption is assigned to the productive catalytic cycle.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) reported approximately 24% alanine and 43% lactate using powdered Aletai meteorite containing about 18 mol% Ni. Removing H2 abolished product formation, supporting an externally H2-driven catalytic interpretation rather than sacrificial-metal reduction. This is a distinct tested catalyst/resource pathway, not a claim that meteoritic and pure-Ni catalysts have interchangeable rates. Natural accessible surface area, reduced-nitrogen activity, and upstream pyruvate supply remain limiting.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"ZnS photocatalytic pyruvate reductive amination with sulfite electron donation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Illuminated sphalerite, ZnS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 303 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"ZnS photocatalyst\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 9, ammonia/ammonium present, sulfite as a sacrificial hole scavenger, and several hours of illumination. Use an anoxic, shallow, UV-accessible pool or wetted mineral film; this cannot operate in a dark deep vent. UV must excite ZnS, approximately wavelengths shorter than its near-UV absorption edge. Natural photon flux, optical attenuation, sulfite persistence, and catalyst photocorrosion require measurement.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formal terminal balance: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 2 SO3(2-) -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 SO4(2-), driven by light. ZnS photoelectrons reduce the carbonyl/ammonia-derived intermediate while sulfite supplies reducing equivalents through hole scavenging. Sulfate represents the fully oxidized sulfur endpoint; the equation does not claim a measured exclusive sulfur-product distribution.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Wang et al. (2012) demonstrated light- and ZnS-dependent keto-acid amination, including pyruvate to alanine, around pH 9 and 30 degrees C, with sulfite as a possible sacrificial donor. Explicit sulfite prevents treating illumination alone as an electron source. The supplied report does not resolve sulfur-product accounting, so sulfate closure remains a formal terminal model. Sulfite oxidation can obtain oxygen atoms from water-derived chemistry without molecular O2. No L selectivity was established.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydroxylamine and metallic-iron conversion of pyruvate to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_023\", \"mol_025\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_024\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Metallic iron powder or reactive grains as a stoichiometric reductant\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Proposed screening window 323-343 K; the supplied report does not specify the amination subexperiment's exact temperature\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"The reported nitrogen-incorporation experiment used six equivalents of hydroxylamine hydrochloride and ten equivalents of Fe0. A mildly acidic, anoxic mineral pore, provisionally pH 4-6, is proposed to retain aqueous Fe2+ and support metal dissolution; that pH window is an implementation hypothesis, not a quoted optimum. Hydroxylamine is an external simple feedstock whose accumulation and persistence must be independently justified. Fe0 requires native or meteoritic metal, not merely abundant dissolved Fe2+.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced four-electron-per-alanine reference: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH2OH + 4 Fe + 8 H+ -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 4 Fe2+ + 4 H2O. Carbonyl condensation introduces an N-O-containing intermediate, followed by reduction of the C=N/N-O functionality. The equation records the minimum productive electron demand; experimental iron excess also permits corrosion and other competing reactions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) observed alanine after adding hydroxylamine hydrochloride and Fe0 to keto-acid chemistry. This supports the transformation family, but the proposed environmental temperature/pH window and sustained dilute operation are speculative because those details are not established by the supplied summary. The molecular formula and InChI here both describe hydroxylamine, H3NO. Fe2+ is explicit rather than replacing consumable Fe0 with an unspecified mineral catalyst. Hydroxylamine supply, iron passivation, and compatibility with transferred pyruvate remain unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde Strecker synthesis of aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"303-333 K as a proposed receiving-pool window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic aqueous condensate with formaldehyde, HCN/CN-, and ammonia/ammonium, provisionally pH 8.5-10. Rapid reaction after discharge capture reduces dependence on storing volatile HCN. Competing formaldehyde condensation and cyanohydrin chemistry reduce the available glycine-precursor inventory. No concentration of 350 mM glycine is inferred from this source step.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced net reaction: HCHO + NH3 + HCN -> NH2CH2CN + H2O. Formaldehyde/ammonia condensation followed by cyanide addition produces aminoacetonitrile. The glycine precursor is achiral.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller (1955, 1957) supports formaldehyde/HCN-derived glycine within the discharge/Strecker framework. Including this branch supplies the organic nitrogen donor required by transamination instead of assuming an unexplained glycine pool. The proposed natural capture conditions and source flux remain conditional, and the chemistry does not establish the highly concentrated glycine inventory used by Stubbs et al. (2020).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoacetonitrile hydrolysis supplying glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_027\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Br\u00f8nsted acid, regenerated during hydrolysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K, proposed warm hydrolysis pocket\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"H+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic, UV-shielded geothermal water, provisionally pH 1-3, following rather than coinciding with alkaline cyanide capture. Residence time depends on nitrile hydrolysis kinetics and may extend beyond days. Transfer to rxn_015 requires conditioning to pH 5 and independent accumulation of glycine; simple hydrolysis does not supply that concentration increase.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced net reaction: NH2CH2CN + 2 H2O -> NH2CH2CO2H + NH3. Sequential nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis produce glycine and release the nitrile nitrogen as ammonia/ammonium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller (1957) supports aminonitrile hydrolysis as a source of amino acids in aqueous discharge products. The environmental hydrolysis window is a proposed implementation, not evidence that natural dilute acidic water reproduces analytical strong-acid workups. This node establishes the chemical ancestry of glycine but does not establish its recovery, concentration, or compatibility with the downstream alum experiment.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Alum-promoted nonenzymatic transamination from glycine to pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_027\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_028\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Potassium alum, KAl(SO4)2\u00b712H2O, approximately 0.1 equivalent relative to glycine\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Alum-derived Al(III)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Experimental benchmark: approximately 350 mM glycine, 1.2 equivalents of pyruvate, pH 5, and 24 hours. Proposed physical setting: a concentrated, acidic geothermal pool containing soluble alum-derived Al(III). These activities require approximately 420 mM pyruvate, around 21000 times the reported upstream concentration. No experimentally validated natural concentration, selective trapping, or flux-matching process is supplied by the cited studies.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced racemic-reference equation: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH2CH2CO2H -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 OHC-CO2H. Condensation and proton-transfer chemistry through nitrogen-linked intermediates transfers the amino group from glycine to pyruvate, with glyoxylate as the oxidized donor product. This transfers existing organic nitrogen rather than fixing new nitrogen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Stubbs et al. (2020) reported approximately 19% alanine after 24 hours under the specified concentrated conditions. The alanine-forming experiment used alum and must not be described as literally metal-free. Neither enzymes nor pyridoxal phosphate are introduced here. This is a supported terminal reaction with an especially severe, explicitly unresolved precursor-accumulation requirement; dilute upstream supplies are not assigned the reported yield. No experimental L bias was established.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing acetaldehyde cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_029\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"378 K in the reported acetylene-derived reaction matrix\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Confined aqueous conditions maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 3 in the experimentally observed competing branch. Free ammonia is suppressed relative to the alkaline Strecker regime. This node can occur in the same acetylene/NiS-derived fluid before an alkaline shift; NiS is not assigned as the catalyst for the cyanohydrin addition itself.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced net reaction: CH3CHO + HCN -> CH3CH(OH)CN. Cyanide addition to the carbonyl followed by protonation traps the C2 aldehyde as a C3 hydroxy nitrile rather than an amino nitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The Communications Chemistry acetylene/NiS study (2023; author names unavailable in the supplied report) observed approximately 27 mM acetaldehyde and 10.2 mM lactonitrile at pH 3, with no alanine. Miller (1957) also distinguished amino- and hydroxynitrile pathways. This experimentally motivated sink makes pH and nitrogen availability explicit instead of directing all aldehyde carbon into alanine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactonitrile hydrolysis to the competing lactate pool\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_029\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_030\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Br\u00f8nsted acid, regenerated during hydrolysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"378-393 K as a proposed hydrolysis window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Confined pressure sufficient to retain liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"H+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic, anoxic pore water, provisionally pH 1-3, with extended residence time. Lactonitrile may also dissociate back to acetaldehyde and HCN; this node represents the hydrolytic sink, not quantitative irreversible conversion. The supplied report does not give a measured environmental rate for this particular hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced net reaction: CH3CH(OH)CN + 2 H2O -> CH3CH(OH)CO2H + NH3. Nitrile hydration and hydrolysis preserve the alpha-hydroxyl group and yield lactate rather than alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller (1957) supports hydroxynitrile hydrolysis as the origin of competing hydroxy acids, and the supplied research identifies lactonitrile as the acetaldehyde branch toward lactic acid. The stated temperature/pH implementation is proposed rather than experimentally established in that report. No unsupported conversion of lactate back into alanine is added.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing H2-driven pyruvate hydrogenation to lactate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_030\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Metallic Ni or exposed meteoritic Fe-Ni surface, matching rxn_009 or rxn_010\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni-containing metal surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Near-neutral anoxic mineral pores, approximately pH 7-8, with hours-to-day contact. This is a competing reaction in the same physical compartments as H2-driven amination, not a separate invented transport stage. Ammonia activity and catalyst identity influence competition between carbonyl hydrogenation and imine reduction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced net reaction: C3H4O3 + H2 -> C3H6O3. Metal-activated hydrogen reduces the pyruvate carbonyl directly, bypassing nitrogen incorporation and producing lactate with unspecified stereochemistry.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) reported substantial lactate alongside alanine, including approximately 43% lactate in the Aletai meteorite experiment. Novikov and Copley (2013) likewise emphasized competing pyruvate sinks and mineral-dependent reaction landscapes. Explicit inclusion prevents treating all fixed pyruvate as available for alanine and does not count the sink as an additional productive pathway.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-fed discharge and acetaldehyde Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"A local reducing discharge co-supplies acetaldehyde and HCN, followed by aqueous alaninonitrile formation and hydrolysis. L-alanine is formed together with D-alanine, without separation. Volatile capture and hydrolysis residence time are the main coupling requirements.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"N2-activating discharge and acetaldehyde Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Energetic activation of N2 replaces an initially ammonia-fed atmosphere in the precursor-source stage. Subsequent carbonyl/cyanide chemistry converges on alaninonitrile and the unresolved L/D-alanine pool. This pathway has lower demonstrated source efficiency and incompletely quantified reduced-nitrogen speciation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene-NiS carbonyl supply followed by Strecker chemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetylene hydration supplies the acetaldehyde used in this branch; rxn_001 supplies cyanide, with ammonia also available as a starting resource. The reaction sequence lists both precursor-supply branches before their convergence and does not imply that discharge-derived acetaldehyde must be consumed. Confined cooling and prompt chemical trapping are required to retain the hydrothermal aldehyde. The demonstrated overall alanine yield was low.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2 fixation followed by ferrous-hydroxide reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Ni3Fe-mediated CO2/H2 chemistry supplies pyruvate for reaction with ammonia and fresh Fe(OH)2 in a warmer alkaline sediment pocket. Fe(OH)2 is consumable, and its ferric product is represented only by formal FeOOH equivalents. L-alanine occurs in the unresolved product mixture. Upstream-to-downstream concentration and rate coupling are not demonstrated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2 fixation followed by mixed-valence iron-precipitate amination\",\n      \"description\": \"CO2-derived pyruvate enters a long-residence, alkaline Fe(II)/Fe(III) sediment-like compartment containing ammonia. The experimental support concerns batch precipitates, not productive flowing vent chimneys. Initial ferric material is an external mineral boundary condition, and neither its natural supply nor the necessary precursor accumulation is established.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2 fixation followed by Ni/H2 catalytic reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Cool pressurized CO2/H2 fixation is followed by near-neutral Ni-catalyzed amination using externally maintained H2 and ammonia. This is an abiotic mineral-pore pathway despite the Biochemical environment label. The two reactions are individually supported, but the 0.02 mM versus 30 mM pyruvate benchmarks cannot be coupled by assuming unchanged yields.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2 fixation followed by meteoritic Fe-Ni/H2 catalytic amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Pyruvate formed by CO2 fixation contacts exposed meteoritic Fe-Ni in an H2-rich confined aqueous compartment. H2 supplies reducing equivalents; the meteorite is catalytic in the demonstrated productive reaction. Accessible surface area, passivation, lactate competition, and dilute precursor supply prevent direct scaling of the reported laboratory yield.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2 fixation followed by illuminated ZnS amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Aqueous pyruvate is transferred from a pressurized fixation zone to an anoxic illuminated pool containing ammonia, ZnS, and sulfite. Advection and pressure release are physically possible but do not establish pyruvate recovery or concentration. Sulfite provides the explicit sacrificial electron source; the product contains both alanine enantiomers.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2 fixation followed by hydroxylamine/Fe0 nitrogen incorporation\",\n      \"description\": \"CO2-derived pyruvate is subjected to hydroxylamine condensation and metallic-iron reduction. Hydroxylamine and reactive Fe0 are explicit external simple resources rather than unexplained catalysts. Their natural abundance, persistence, and compatibility with dilute pyruvate remain unresolved, and the proposed environmental implementation is distinguished from the demonstrated reaction family.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Independent glycine and pyruvate supply followed by alum-assisted transamination\",\n      \"description\": \"Discharge/Strecker chemistry supplies glycine while CO2/H2 fixation supplies pyruvate; the two branches converge only at transamination. Glycine donates existing organic nitrogen, producing glyoxylate and an unresolved L/D-alanine mixture. The demonstrated 350 mM glycine and approximately 420 mM pyruvate conditions are retained as demanding benchmarks, not claimed natural concentrations. No measured separation or concentration process currently closes this pathway quantitatively.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_026\", \"mol_027\", \"mol_015\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_015\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_018\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_030\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Alanine", "overall_strategy": "Ten distinct pathways form L-alanine molecules as a component of an unresolved alanine enantiomer mixture. No isolation, L enrichment, enantiopurity, or explanation of biological homochirality is claimed. Every alanine-forming reaction explicitly produces separate L- and D-alanine nodes; equal production is an achiral-model expectation where experimental enantiomeric analysis is unavailable. Three pathways use discharge-derived or acetylene-derived acetaldehyde followed by Strecker chemistry. Seven use CO2/H2-derived pyruvate followed by mineral-promoted reductive amination, photocatalysis, hydroxylamine reduction, or glycine-dependent transamination. These are alternative, staged settings, not a proposed one-pot reactor. Reaction-level conditions distinguish experimental benchmarks from proposed environmental implementations. Crucially, the literature does not demonstrate quantitative end-to-end coupling: approximately 0.02 mM upstream pyruvate contrasts with 30 mM in the Ni/H2 benchmark and approximately 420 mM in the transamination benchmark. These correspond to concentration factors of approximately 1500 and 21000. Nonselective evaporation is not an established solution: concentrating the reported 26.72 mM formate coproduct 1500-fold would nominally require approximately 40 M formate. Selective accumulation, sustained dilute conversion, or other separation would therefore require independent demonstration. Without additional carbon accumulation, 0.02 mM pyruvate permits at most 0.01 mM L-alanine under a racemic, complete-conversion model. Reported downstream yields are not propagated through these unvalidated couplings. All settings are anoxic; ferric phases and sulfate do not imply molecular oxygen. Neutral carboxylic-acid and ammonia formulas are reference forms for pH-dependent aqueous speciation. Non-alanine stereochemistry is unspecified. An environment_formed value of null denotes an external resource or formation in multiple environments. The schema's intermediate role also includes coproducts and competitive sinks. Balanced equations appear in mechanism fields without repeating IDs in input or output arrays; equations for lumped source reactions are bookkeeping examples, not measured product distributions.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "methane", "common_name": "methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "N2", "iupac_name": "dinitrogen", "common_name": "nitrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/N2/c1-2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "acetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C2H2", "iupac_name": "ethyne", "common_name": "acetylene", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "CO", "iupac_name": "carbon monoxide", "common_name": "carbon monoxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO/c1-2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "alaninonitrile, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c1-3(5)2-4/h3H,5H2,1H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "L-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2R)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "D-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m1/s1", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c1-2-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "CH2O2", "iupac_name": "methanoic acid", "common_name": "formic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O2/c2-1-3/h1H,(H,2,3)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "ethanoic acid", "common_name": "acetic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c1-2(3)4/h1H3,(H,3,4)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "FeH2O2", "iupac_name": "iron(II) hydroxide", "common_name": "ferrous hydroxide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "FeHO2", "iupac_name": "iron(III) oxide hydroxide", "common_name": "hydrous ferric solid represented as FeOOH equivalents", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "O3S-2", "iupac_name": "sulfite", "common_name": "sulfite ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O3S/c1-4(2)3/h(H2,1,2,3)/p-2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "O4S-2", "iupac_name": "sulfate", "common_name": "sulfate ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O4S/c1-5(2,3)4/h(H2,1,2,3,4)/p-2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "iron", "common_name": "metallic iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_024", "formula": "Fe+2", "iupac_name": "iron(2+)", "common_name": "ferrous ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_025", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "hydron", "common_name": "proton", "inchi": "InChI=1S/p+1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_026", "formula": "C2H4N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoethanenitrile", "common_name": "aminoacetonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4N2/c3-1-2-4/h1,3H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_027", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoethanoic acid", "common_name": "glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_028", "formula": "C2H2O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxoethanoic acid", "common_name": "glyoxylic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_029", "formula": "C3H5NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanenitrile", "common_name": "lactonitrile, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H5NO/c1-3(5)2-4/h3,5H,1H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_030", "formula": "C3H6O3", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanoic acid", "common_name": "lactic acid, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2,4H,1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Ammonia-fed reducing discharge supplying cyanide and carbonyl compounds", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_001", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 293-330 K in the receiving water; the electrical discharge is nonthermal", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar in a circulating laboratory analogue", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic CH4/NH3/H2/water-vapor gas circulation with repeated electrical discharge and aqueous capture over approximately one week. Proposed field analogue: a transient reducing volcanic plume over a cool receiving pool, not a globally methane/ammonia-dominated atmosphere. Rapid condensation, dissolution, and subsequent chemical trapping are required; a partly enclosed pore or basin limits volatile losses. H2 is co-fed and returned, with possible net production."}, "mechanism": "Lumped radical/plasma source reaction, not an elementary mechanism. One balanced bookkeeping example is 4 CH4 + NH3 + 2 H2O + H2 -> CH3CHO + HCHO + HCN + 9 H2. Parallel radical reactions generate cyanide and carbonyl compounds before aqueous Strecker chemistry. The coefficients do not specify experimental product ratios, and numerous untracked minor products also form.", "reasoning": "Miller (1955, 1957) supports discharge production of aldehydes and HCN followed by aminonitrile chemistry; the resulting alanine was DL. Johnson et al. (2008) supports a volcanic-discharge analogue. Trail et al. (2011) argues against assuming a persistently strongly reducing global atmosphere, so this node requires a local or episodic reduced-gas source. No iron catalyst is assigned because Miller (1957) did not find a material advantage from added ferrous salt. Capture efficiency and natural production flux remain unquantified."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Nitrogen-fed reducing discharge with energetic nitrogen activation", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_005", "mol_001", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_003", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 293-330 K in condensate; nonthermal discharge in the gas phase", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar in the experimental analogue", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic CH4/N2/H2/water-vapor mixture with repeated energetic discharge and cool aqueous capture. Nitrogen fixation is driven by discharge energy, not by thermal equilibrium at the condensate temperature. Use the same short-transfer, low-headspace capture concept as rxn_001. The experiment's lower yield must not be replaced by the ammonia-fed source yield."}, "mechanism": "Lumped nitrogen-activation and carbonyl-source node. An illustrative balanced equation is 4 CH4 + N2 + 2 H2O + H2 -> CH3CHO + HCHO + HCN + NH3 + 6 H2. Discharge activation of N2 enables formation of cyanide and reduced nitrogen, while methane/water chemistry supplies carbonyl compounds. This equation is an elemental accounting example, not a measured distribution or a resolved sequence of intermediates.", "reasoning": "Miller (1957) obtained the same general amino-acid product family when N2 replaced initially supplied NH3, although at lower yield. This establishes energetic nitrogen entry, but the supplied report does not quantify the individual ammonia and aldehyde source fluxes represented here. Their partitioning is therefore an incompletely characterized lumped-source model. The route does not assume that ordinary UV alone performs the same chemistry, and it retains the reduced-carbon and volatile-capture limitations."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "NiS-mediated acetylene hydration supplying acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_010"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Freshly precipitated nickel sulfide, NiS"], "conditions": {"temperature": "378 K", "pressure": "Confined aqueous reactor above water's vapor pressure at 378 K; acetylene and CO gas activities maintained", "catalyst": "NiS", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acetylene and CO contact fresh NiS in a sealed, shallow hydrothermal pore or reactor. The supplied report does not establish natural gas partial pressures. CO is retained as an experimentally relevant feed/conditioning gas but is not assigned net consumption in the hydration subreaction. Downstream use requires cooling and aqueous transfer through confined fractures, or reagent addition before substantial acetaldehyde escape."}, "mechanism": "Balanced hydration subreaction: C2H2 + H2O + CO -> CH3CHO + CO. NiS promotes hydration of acetylene, with enol-to-aldehyde conversion furnishing acetaldehyde. CO appears on both sides because its presence in the tested reaction matrix is retained without inventing carbon incorporation into this isolated C2 transformation.", "reasoning": "The acetylene/NiS study in Communications Chemistry (2023; author names are not given in the supplied report) demonstrated millimolar acetaldehyde at 105 degrees C; catalyst-free controls gave less than approximately 40 micromolar, and FeS alone did not provide detectable free acetaldehyde. Subsequent cyanide/ammonium addition gave only approximately 180 micromolar alanine after seven days. Thus acetaldehyde formation is supported, but gas supply, volatile retention, pH sequencing, and overall alanine efficiency remain limiting."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Acetaldehyde Strecker conversion to alaninonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "303-333 K as a proposed concentrated-pool operating window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Buffered, anoxic water around pH 8.5-10 with sufficient NH3/NH4+ relative to carbonyl and cyanide. Use short aqueous transfer from discharge condensate, or cooled NiS-pore effluent. Aldehyde and HCN should be chemically trapped before extensive open-pool evaporation. The exact natural residence time and conversion at dilute concentrations are not established."}, "mechanism": "Balanced net reaction: CH3CHO + NH3 + HCN -> CH3CH(NH2)CN + H2O. Ammonia addition gives an aminoalcohol, dehydration gives an imine/iminium species, and cyanide addition forms alaninonitrile. Achiral reagents do not specify the configuration at the newly formed stereocenter.", "reasoning": "Miller (1957) supports the aldehyde/HCN/aminonitrile mechanism. The Communications Chemistry acetylene/NiS study (2023) detected 1-aminoethan-1-ol and 2-aminopropanenitrile after restoring alkaline conditions, providing direct intermediate evidence. The proposed cooler surface window is an environmental implementation rather than a quoted optimum from that hydrothermal study. Low ammonia activity or acidic conditions divert carbon toward lactonitrile, represented by rxn_016."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Alaninonitrile hydrolysis producing both alanine enantiomers", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Br\u00f8nsted acid, regenerated during hydrolysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-370 K, proposed warm hydrolysis pocket", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "H+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Aqueous, UV-shielded acidic geothermal pocket, provisionally pH 1-3, reached after aminonitrile formation rather than during cyanide capture. Hydrolysis proceeds over days or longer depending on acidity and temperature. This is not a claim that mild natural water reproduces rapid laboratory strong-acid hydrolysis. Product removal to cooler water limits prolonged thermal exposure."}, "mechanism": "Balanced racemic-reference equation: 2 CH3CH(NH2)CN + 4 H2O -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 NH3. Nitrile hydration proceeds through an amide and then carboxylic acid formation. The two product nodes describe molecules in the same unresolved solution, not a separation operation.", "reasoning": "Miller (1955) identified DL-alanine, and Miller (1957) showed that aminonitrile hydrolysis could account for amino-acid accumulation on approximately week-long experimental timescales. The specific natural pH/temperature window proposed here requires kinetic validation and is not presented as a measured optimum. Equal enantiomer production is appropriate for an achiral Strecker source; no L-selective process or isolated L product is inferred."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Ni3Fe-catalyzed CO2 hydrogenation supplying dilute pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni3Fe alloy particles, an awaruite-like catalytic material"], "conditions": {"temperature": "298 K", "pressure": "25 bar total CO2/H2", "catalyst": "Ni3Fe", "aqueous": true, "other": "Experimental benchmark: approximately pH 6, CO2:H2 gas ratio 3:2, 24 hours. Physical setting: cool, pressurized, serpentinization-fed mineral pores, not a 350-600 K vent interior and not an enzyme-containing system. Downstream fluid transfer requires retained pyruvate, a change in gas activity and pH, and suitable residence time. Neither selective pyruvate concentration nor coupled alanine synthesis was demonstrated in this experiment."}, "mechanism": "The pyruvate branch has the balanced net equation 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> C3H4O3 + 3 H2O. Parallel formate and acetate formation are CO2 + H2 -> CH2O2 and 2 CO2 + 4 H2 -> C2H4O2 + 2 H2O. Their illustrative sum is 6 CO2 + 10 H2 -> C3H4O3 + CH2O2 + C2H4O2 + 5 H2O. This sum does not imply equal product fluxes or a single elementary catalytic cycle.", "reasoning": "Beyazay et al. (2023) measured approximately 26.72 mM formate, 0.04 mM acetate, and 0.02 mM pyruvate under this benchmark. These observations justify the carbon-source connection but identify pyruvate as a minor product. Kelley et al. (2005) supports H2-rich serpentinizing fluids as a resource analogue, not these exact reactor conditions. The roughly 1500-fold concentration difference from the Kaur et al. (2024) amination benchmark is an unresolved integration problem, not proof that dilute conversion is impossible."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Fresh ferrous-hydroxide-promoted pyruvate reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003", "mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_001", "mol_019"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Fresh Fe(OH)2 as a consumable reducing solid, not a trace recyclable catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-348 K, within the reported warm aqueous experimental series", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, UV-shielded alkaline spring sediment or confined mineral slurry near pH 9. Fresh precipitate, substantial mineral excess, excess NH3/NH4+, and hours-to-day contact are required experimental features. The supplied report does not give a separate optimized Fe(OH)2-only recipe. Transfer from rxn_006 requires pH adjustment by anoxic fluid mixing; conversion of its approximately 0.02 mM pyruvate effluent has not been measured."}, "mechanism": "Formal Fe(II)-to-Fe(III) balance: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 4 Fe(OH)2 -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 H2O + 4 FeOOH. Pyruvate/ammonia condensation is followed by reduction of the C=N functionality. FeOOH denotes oxidation-state and hydration-equivalent accounting for a hydrous ferric product, not identification of a particular mineral phase.", "reasoning": "Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003) demonstrated reductive amination with freshly precipitated FeS or Fe(OH)2. Their approximately 52 mol% alanine maximum was reported for the FeS system and is not transferred to this Fe(OH)2 node. Carbon-to-alanine conversion has experimental support; the exact spent-solid composition is not established in the supplied evidence, so the FeOOH product representation is explicitly provisional bookkeeping. No unsupported pyrite coproduct or catalytic regeneration cycle is asserted."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Mixed-valence iron-precipitate-promoted pyruvate reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003", "mol_018", "mol_019"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_001", "mol_019"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Fresh mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxide-oxyhydroxide precipitate with approximately 1:1 initial Fe(II):Fe(III)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Mixed-valence iron precipitate; its Fe(II) reducing inventory is consumable", "aqueous": true, "other": "Experimental benchmark: pH 10, approximately 1:1 Fe(II):Fe(III), and 48-72 hours in closed batch-like conditions with excess ammonia. Field analogue: a long-residence, anoxic, mineral-rich spring sediment pore rather than a rapidly flowing chimney. Initial ferric material is an external mineral resource; its local production or delivery under anoxia must be established independently."}, "mechanism": "A balanced bookkeeping example retaining an initial ferric inventory is 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 4 Fe(OH)2 + 4 FeOOH -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 H2O + 8 FeOOH. Mixed-valence surfaces influence condensation and reduction selectivity. The FeOOH formula represents hydrous ferric equivalents, not an experimentally resolved transformation into a specific crystalline phase.", "reasoning": "Barge et al. (2019) found approximately 70% alanine and 30% lactate among analyzed liquid organic products under favorable warm, alkaline batch conditions. Fully ferric material was inactive, while strongly ferrous material favored lactate. Their chimney experiments did not detect alanine, and ammonia excess was acknowledged as a limitation. Accordingly, neither the batch product fraction nor the provisional solid-phase balance is treated as a demonstrated flowing-environment yield. Anoxic availability of the initial mixed-valence material and coupling to dilute upstream pyruvate remain conditional."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Nickel-catalyzed H2-driven pyruvate reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Accessible metallic nickel surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni", "aqueous": true, "other": "Experimental benchmark: pH 7-8, 30 mM pyruvate, 6-150 mM ammonium, 24 hours. Physical setting: cool, confined, anoxic mineral pores receiving H2-rich water; no enzymes or membranes are required. Transfer from the CO2-fixation zone must change CO2/H2 activity and pH without losing pyruvate. Sustained gas pressure and 24-hour residence are boundary conditions, not assumptions about an open surface pool."}, "mechanism": "Balanced racemic-reference equation: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 2 H2 -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 H2O. Nickel activates H2 and promotes hydrogenation of a pyruvate-derived imine/iminium intermediate. Hydrogen is the external electron donor; the metal is catalytic rather than the stoichiometric reductant.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) demonstrated this chemistry and reported up to approximately 17% alanine with Ni at 6 mM ammonia and 30 mM pyruvate, with that yield referenced to ammonia. Nickel is chosen over Pd on geochemical abundance grounds despite Pd's higher laboratory yield. The reported yield is not applied to the approximately 0.02 mM pyruvate from Beyazay et al. (2023). Lactate formation competes and is explicitly represented by rxn_018; an achiral surface gives no established L preference."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Meteoritic Fe-Ni-catalyzed H2-driven pyruvate reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Exposed Fe-Ni iron-meteorite material, represented by the Aletai experimental analogue"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2 in the demonstrated reaction family", "catalyst": "Meteoritic Fe-Ni alloy", "aqueous": true, "other": "Near-neutral anoxic water, approximately pH 7-8, ammonium present, and approximately 24-hour contact. Physical setting: confined water contacting impact-fragmented or otherwise exposed meteoritic metal. Laboratory powders have much greater accessible area than intact meteorites; catalyst area and passivation must be measured before scaling. The dilute upstream pyruvate integration gap is unchanged from rxn_009."}, "mechanism": "Balanced racemic-reference equation: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 2 H2 -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 H2O. Ni-containing alloy sites catalyze H2 activation and imine reduction. No net Fe or Ni consumption is assigned to the productive catalytic cycle.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) reported approximately 24% alanine and 43% lactate using powdered Aletai meteorite containing about 18 mol% Ni. Removing H2 abolished product formation, supporting an externally H2-driven catalytic interpretation rather than sacrificial-metal reduction. This is a distinct tested catalyst/resource pathway, not a claim that meteoritic and pure-Ni catalysts have interchangeable rates. Natural accessible surface area, reduced-nitrogen activity, and upstream pyruvate supply remain limiting."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "ZnS photocatalytic pyruvate reductive amination with sulfite electron donation", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003", "mol_020"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_021"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Illuminated sphalerite, ZnS"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 303 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "ZnS photocatalyst", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 9, ammonia/ammonium present, sulfite as a sacrificial hole scavenger, and several hours of illumination. Use an anoxic, shallow, UV-accessible pool or wetted mineral film; this cannot operate in a dark deep vent. UV must excite ZnS, approximately wavelengths shorter than its near-UV absorption edge. Natural photon flux, optical attenuation, sulfite persistence, and catalyst photocorrosion require measurement."}, "mechanism": "Formal terminal balance: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 2 SO3(2-) -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 SO4(2-), driven by light. ZnS photoelectrons reduce the carbonyl/ammonia-derived intermediate while sulfite supplies reducing equivalents through hole scavenging. Sulfate represents the fully oxidized sulfur endpoint; the equation does not claim a measured exclusive sulfur-product distribution.", "reasoning": "Wang et al. (2012) demonstrated light- and ZnS-dependent keto-acid amination, including pyruvate to alanine, around pH 9 and 30 degrees C, with sulfite as a possible sacrificial donor. Explicit sulfite prevents treating illumination alone as an electron source. The supplied report does not resolve sulfur-product accounting, so sulfate closure remains a formal terminal model. Sulfite oxidation can obtain oxygen atoms from water-derived chemistry without molecular O2. No L selectivity was established."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Hydroxylamine and metallic-iron conversion of pyruvate to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_022", "mol_023", "mol_025"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_024", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Metallic iron powder or reactive grains as a stoichiometric reductant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Proposed screening window 323-343 K; the supplied report does not specify the amination subexperiment's exact temperature", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "The reported nitrogen-incorporation experiment used six equivalents of hydroxylamine hydrochloride and ten equivalents of Fe0. A mildly acidic, anoxic mineral pore, provisionally pH 4-6, is proposed to retain aqueous Fe2+ and support metal dissolution; that pH window is an implementation hypothesis, not a quoted optimum. Hydroxylamine is an external simple feedstock whose accumulation and persistence must be independently justified. Fe0 requires native or meteoritic metal, not merely abundant dissolved Fe2+."}, "mechanism": "Balanced four-electron-per-alanine reference: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH2OH + 4 Fe + 8 H+ -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 4 Fe2+ + 4 H2O. Carbonyl condensation introduces an N-O-containing intermediate, followed by reduction of the C=N/N-O functionality. The equation records the minimum productive electron demand; experimental iron excess also permits corrosion and other competing reactions.", "reasoning": "Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) observed alanine after adding hydroxylamine hydrochloride and Fe0 to keto-acid chemistry. This supports the transformation family, but the proposed environmental temperature/pH window and sustained dilute operation are speculative because those details are not established by the supplied summary. The molecular formula and InChI here both describe hydroxylamine, H3NO. Fe2+ is explicit rather than replacing consumable Fe0 with an unspecified mineral catalyst. Hydroxylamine supply, iron passivation, and compatibility with transferred pyruvate remain unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Formaldehyde Strecker synthesis of aminoacetonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_026", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "303-333 K as a proposed receiving-pool window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic aqueous condensate with formaldehyde, HCN/CN-, and ammonia/ammonium, provisionally pH 8.5-10. Rapid reaction after discharge capture reduces dependence on storing volatile HCN. Competing formaldehyde condensation and cyanohydrin chemistry reduce the available glycine-precursor inventory. No concentration of 350 mM glycine is inferred from this source step."}, "mechanism": "Balanced net reaction: HCHO + NH3 + HCN -> NH2CH2CN + H2O. Formaldehyde/ammonia condensation followed by cyanide addition produces aminoacetonitrile. The glycine precursor is achiral.", "reasoning": "Miller (1955, 1957) supports formaldehyde/HCN-derived glycine within the discharge/Strecker framework. Including this branch supplies the organic nitrogen donor required by transamination instead of assuming an unexplained glycine pool. The proposed natural capture conditions and source flux remain conditional, and the chemistry does not establish the highly concentrated glycine inventory used by Stubbs et al. (2020)."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Aminoacetonitrile hydrolysis supplying glycine", "inputs": ["mol_026", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_027", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Br\u00f8nsted acid, regenerated during hydrolysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-370 K, proposed warm hydrolysis pocket", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "H+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic, UV-shielded geothermal water, provisionally pH 1-3, following rather than coinciding with alkaline cyanide capture. Residence time depends on nitrile hydrolysis kinetics and may extend beyond days. Transfer to rxn_015 requires conditioning to pH 5 and independent accumulation of glycine; simple hydrolysis does not supply that concentration increase."}, "mechanism": "Balanced net reaction: NH2CH2CN + 2 H2O -> NH2CH2CO2H + NH3. Sequential nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis produce glycine and release the nitrile nitrogen as ammonia/ammonium.", "reasoning": "Miller (1957) supports aminonitrile hydrolysis as a source of amino acids in aqueous discharge products. The environmental hydrolysis window is a proposed implementation, not evidence that natural dilute acidic water reproduces analytical strong-acid workups. This node establishes the chemical ancestry of glycine but does not establish its recovery, concentration, or compatibility with the downstream alum experiment."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Alum-promoted nonenzymatic transamination from glycine to pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_027"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_028"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Potassium alum, KAl(SO4)2\u00b712H2O, approximately 0.1 equivalent relative to glycine"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Alum-derived Al(III)", "aqueous": true, "other": "Experimental benchmark: approximately 350 mM glycine, 1.2 equivalents of pyruvate, pH 5, and 24 hours. Proposed physical setting: a concentrated, acidic geothermal pool containing soluble alum-derived Al(III). These activities require approximately 420 mM pyruvate, around 21000 times the reported upstream concentration. No experimentally validated natural concentration, selective trapping, or flux-matching process is supplied by the cited studies."}, "mechanism": "Balanced racemic-reference equation: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH2CH2CO2H -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 OHC-CO2H. Condensation and proton-transfer chemistry through nitrogen-linked intermediates transfers the amino group from glycine to pyruvate, with glyoxylate as the oxidized donor product. This transfers existing organic nitrogen rather than fixing new nitrogen.", "reasoning": "Stubbs et al. (2020) reported approximately 19% alanine after 24 hours under the specified concentrated conditions. The alanine-forming experiment used alum and must not be described as literally metal-free. Neither enzymes nor pyridoxal phosphate are introduced here. This is a supported terminal reaction with an especially severe, explicitly unresolved precursor-accumulation requirement; dilute upstream supplies are not assigned the reported yield. No experimental L bias was established."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Competing acetaldehyde cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_029"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "378 K in the reported acetylene-derived reaction matrix", "pressure": "Confined aqueous conditions maintaining liquid water", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 3 in the experimentally observed competing branch. Free ammonia is suppressed relative to the alkaline Strecker regime. This node can occur in the same acetylene/NiS-derived fluid before an alkaline shift; NiS is not assigned as the catalyst for the cyanohydrin addition itself."}, "mechanism": "Balanced net reaction: CH3CHO + HCN -> CH3CH(OH)CN. Cyanide addition to the carbonyl followed by protonation traps the C2 aldehyde as a C3 hydroxy nitrile rather than an amino nitrile.", "reasoning": "The Communications Chemistry acetylene/NiS study (2023; author names unavailable in the supplied report) observed approximately 27 mM acetaldehyde and 10.2 mM lactonitrile at pH 3, with no alanine. Miller (1957) also distinguished amino- and hydroxynitrile pathways. This experimentally motivated sink makes pH and nitrogen availability explicit instead of directing all aldehyde carbon into alanine."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Lactonitrile hydrolysis to the competing lactate pool", "inputs": ["mol_029", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_030", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Br\u00f8nsted acid, regenerated during hydrolysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "378-393 K as a proposed hydrolysis window", "pressure": "Confined pressure sufficient to retain liquid water", "catalyst": "H+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic, anoxic pore water, provisionally pH 1-3, with extended residence time. Lactonitrile may also dissociate back to acetaldehyde and HCN; this node represents the hydrolytic sink, not quantitative irreversible conversion. The supplied report does not give a measured environmental rate for this particular hydrolysis."}, "mechanism": "Balanced net reaction: CH3CH(OH)CN + 2 H2O -> CH3CH(OH)CO2H + NH3. Nitrile hydration and hydrolysis preserve the alpha-hydroxyl group and yield lactate rather than alanine.", "reasoning": "Miller (1957) supports hydroxynitrile hydrolysis as the origin of competing hydroxy acids, and the supplied research identifies lactonitrile as the acetaldehyde branch toward lactic acid. The stated temperature/pH implementation is proposed rather than experimentally established in that report. No unsupported conversion of lactate back into alanine is added."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Competing H2-driven pyruvate hydrogenation to lactate", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_030"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Metallic Ni or exposed meteoritic Fe-Ni surface, matching rxn_009 or rxn_010"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni-containing metal surface", "aqueous": true, "other": "Near-neutral anoxic mineral pores, approximately pH 7-8, with hours-to-day contact. This is a competing reaction in the same physical compartments as H2-driven amination, not a separate invented transport stage. Ammonia activity and catalyst identity influence competition between carbonyl hydrogenation and imine reduction."}, "mechanism": "Balanced net reaction: C3H4O3 + H2 -> C3H6O3. Metal-activated hydrogen reduces the pyruvate carbonyl directly, bypassing nitrogen incorporation and producing lactate with unspecified stereochemistry.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) reported substantial lactate alongside alanine, including approximately 43% lactate in the Aletai meteorite experiment. Novikov and Copley (2013) likewise emphasized competing pyruvate sinks and mineral-dependent reaction landscapes. Explicit inclusion prevents treating all fixed pyruvate as available for alanine and does not count the sink as an additional productive pathway."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Ammonia-fed discharge and acetaldehyde Strecker pathway", "description": "A local reducing discharge co-supplies acetaldehyde and HCN, followed by aqueous alaninonitrile formation and hydrolysis. L-alanine is formed together with D-alanine, without separation. Volatile capture and hydrolysis residence time are the main coupling requirements.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "N2-activating discharge and acetaldehyde Strecker pathway", "description": "Energetic activation of N2 replaces an initially ammonia-fed atmosphere in the precursor-source stage. Subsequent carbonyl/cyanide chemistry converges on alaninonitrile and the unresolved L/D-alanine pool. This pathway has lower demonstrated source efficiency and incompletely quantified reduced-nitrogen speciation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Acetylene-NiS carbonyl supply followed by Strecker chemistry", "description": "Acetylene hydration supplies the acetaldehyde used in this branch; rxn_001 supplies cyanide, with ammonia also available as a starting resource. The reaction sequence lists both precursor-supply branches before their convergence and does not imply that discharge-derived acetaldehyde must be consumed. Confined cooling and prompt chemical trapping are required to retain the hydrothermal aldehyde. The demonstrated overall alanine yield was low.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_006", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "CO2 fixation followed by ferrous-hydroxide reductive amination", "description": "Ni3Fe-mediated CO2/H2 chemistry supplies pyruvate for reaction with ammonia and fresh Fe(OH)2 in a warmer alkaline sediment pocket. Fe(OH)2 is consumable, and its ferric product is represented only by formal FeOOH equivalents. L-alanine occurs in the unresolved product mixture. Upstream-to-downstream concentration and rate coupling are not demonstrated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "CO2 fixation followed by mixed-valence iron-precipitate amination", "description": "CO2-derived pyruvate enters a long-residence, alkaline Fe(II)/Fe(III) sediment-like compartment containing ammonia. The experimental support concerns batch precipitates, not productive flowing vent chimneys. Initial ferric material is an external mineral boundary condition, and neither its natural supply nor the necessary precursor accumulation is established.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "CO2 fixation followed by Ni/H2 catalytic reductive amination", "description": "Cool pressurized CO2/H2 fixation is followed by near-neutral Ni-catalyzed amination using externally maintained H2 and ammonia. This is an abiotic mineral-pore pathway despite the Biochemical environment label. The two reactions are individually supported, but the 0.02 mM versus 30 mM pyruvate benchmarks cannot be coupled by assuming unchanged yields.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "CO2 fixation followed by meteoritic Fe-Ni/H2 catalytic amination", "description": "Pyruvate formed by CO2 fixation contacts exposed meteoritic Fe-Ni in an H2-rich confined aqueous compartment. H2 supplies reducing equivalents; the meteorite is catalytic in the demonstrated productive reaction. Accessible surface area, passivation, lactate competition, and dilute precursor supply prevent direct scaling of the reported laboratory yield.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "CO2 fixation followed by illuminated ZnS amination", "description": "Aqueous pyruvate is transferred from a pressurized fixation zone to an anoxic illuminated pool containing ammonia, ZnS, and sulfite. Advection and pressure release are physically possible but do not establish pyruvate recovery or concentration. Sulfite provides the explicit sacrificial electron source; the product contains both alanine enantiomers.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "CO2 fixation followed by hydroxylamine/Fe0 nitrogen incorporation", "description": "CO2-derived pyruvate is subjected to hydroxylamine condensation and metallic-iron reduction. Hydroxylamine and reactive Fe0 are explicit external simple resources rather than unexplained catalysts. Their natural abundance, persistence, and compatibility with dilute pyruvate remain unresolved, and the proposed environmental implementation is distinguished from the demonstrated reaction family.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Independent glycine and pyruvate supply followed by alum-assisted transamination", "description": "Discharge/Strecker chemistry supplies glycine while CO2/H2 fixation supplies pyruvate; the two branches converge only at transamination. Glycine donates existing organic nitrogen, producing glyoxylate and an unresolved L/D-alanine mixture. The demonstrated 350 mM glycine and approximately 420 mM pyruvate conditions are retained as demanding benchmarks, not claimed natural concentrations. No measured separation or concentration process currently closes this pathway quantitatively.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_006", "rxn_013", "rxn_014", "rxn_015"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_006", "mol_026", "mol_027", "mol_015"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_015"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_006", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_013", "rxn_016"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_007", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_016"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_008", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_013"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_015", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_006"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_015", "rxn_018"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_012", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_015"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_013", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_015"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_030", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_017", "rxn_018"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 544124.4359016418, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T04:22:52.852903", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Alanine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Ten distinct pathways form L-alanine molecules as a component of an unresolved alanine enantiomer mixture. No isolation, L enrichment, enantiopurity, or explanation of biological homochirality is claimed. Every alanine-forming reaction explicitly produces separate L- and D-alanine nodes; equal production is an achiral-model expectation where experimental enantiomeric analysis is unavailable. Three pathways use discharge-derived or acetylene-derived acetaldehyde followed by Strecker chemistry. Seven use CO2/H2-derived pyruvate followed by mineral-promoted reductive amination, photocatalysis, hydroxylamine reduction, or glycine-dependent transamination. These are alternative, staged settings, not a proposed one-pot reactor. Reaction-level conditions distinguish experimental benchmarks from proposed environmental implementations. Crucially, the literature does not demonstrate quantitative end-to-end coupling: approximately 0.02 mM upstream pyruvate contrasts with 30 mM in the Ni/H2 benchmark and approximately 420 mM in the transamination benchmark. These correspond to concentration factors of approximately 1500 and 21000. Nonselective evaporation is not an established solution: concentrating the reported 26.72 mM form