ucts.\",\n        \"Provide hydrolysis pH, temperature and duration, distinguishing environmental production of free alanine from any analytical hydrolysis.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.87,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.96,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.95\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.91,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.9,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.93,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.86,\n      \"feedback\": \"Miller (1957) supports amino-acid precursor production when N2 replaces initially supplied ammonia in a reducing discharge. Energetic nitrogen activation is therefore a supported transformation, not an assumed spontaneous reduction. Lower reported yields do not invalidate production of some L-alanine, but the supplied network does not establish the reduced-nitrogen inventory reaching the aqueous Strecker stage. The lumped source equation appropriately should not be interpreted as a measured product distribution.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify discharge conditions and measured or defensibly estimated NH3, HCN and acetaldehyde production under the selected N2-containing gas mixture.\",\n        \"Track precursor capture, losses and aqueous reaction time rather than transferring ammonia-fed discharge yields to this pathway.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.93,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.96,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.96,\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.93\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.92,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.83,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.7,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.89,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.84,\n      \"feedback\": \"The