ng, ammonia activity, precipitation state and residence time remain unspecified here.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Test reductive amination using approximately 0.02 mM pyruvate in the actual upstream coproduct matrix, measuring alanine formation and competing losses.\",\n        \"Specify fresh Fe(OH)2 generation, stoichiometric mineral demand, ammonia concentration, pH, temperature and contact time.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.9\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.9,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.75,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.68,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.83,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.79,\n      \"feedback\": \"Barge et al. (2019) provide strong support for mixed-valence iron precipitates promoting pyruvate amination in warm alkaline batch systems. Their favorable results depended on mineral redox composition, ammonia excess and long residence times; alanine was not detected in their flowing chimney experiments. A surface sediment implementation is not intrinsically inconsistent, but its relevant conditions and connection to dilute upstream pyruvate are not specified. Ferric material in this anoxic network is not itself a contradiction.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Define the reactive precipitate, Fe(II):Fe(III) ratio, ammonia inventory, alkaline pH and residence time, using the batch results rather than assuming chimney performance.\",\n        \"Measure conversion and lactate competition with dilute fixation effluent, including evolution and passivation of the iron phase.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.95\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.93,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.78,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.85,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.82,\n      \"feedback\": \"Both principal transformations have strong experimental support from Beyazay et al. (2023) and Kaur et al. (2024), and rxn_018 is a valid competing branch rather than an additional synthesis step. The acknowledged 1500-fold pyruvate concentration difference prevents straightforward propagation of the downstream benchmark yield. It does not establish that dilute alanine production is impossible or that concentration is mandatory. The label 'Biochemical' is not a physical specification: H2 pressure, pH adjustment, catalyst accessibility and fluid handling must still be defined.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Demonstrate coupled conversion of the real fixation effluent, or sustained dilute conversion with measured production and loss rates.\",\n        \"Specify the transition between the approximately 25 bar fixation benchmark and 5 bar H2 amination benchmark, including gas retention, pH and nickel surface condition.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.95\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.91,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.75,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.65,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.83,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.79,\n      \"feedback\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) support H2-dependent alanine formation using meteoritic Fe-Ni, with substantial competing lactate formation represented by rxn_018. Treating H2 as the reductant and the meteorite as a catalyst matches the reported controls. Nevertheless, accessible metal area, passivation and H2 availability in the proposed setting are not established by the powdered-meteorite benchmark. The dilute upstream pyruvate coupling remains unvalidated independently of those catalyst questions.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Evaluate naturally plausible fragments or sediments with measured accessible Fe-Ni area and catalyst lifetime rather than assuming powdered-material performance.\",\n        \"Measure alanine versus lactate production from actual dilute fixation effluent under a specified H2 supply.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.9\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.9,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.74,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.67,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.83,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.79,\n      \"feedback\": \"Wang et al. (2012) support illuminated ZnS-mediated amination with a sacrificial electron donor. Explicit sulfite oxidation to sulfate supplies the required reducing equivalents without implying molecular oxygen. The surface assignment permits illumination, but does not establish suitable spectrum, photon flux, optical penetration or sulfite persistence. Delivery of dilute pyruvate from the fixation setting and performance in its coproduct matrix remain proposed rather than demonstrated.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify ZnS surface state, effective illumination spectrum and intensity, water depth, pH, sulfite loading and competing photochemical losses.\",\n        \"Demonstrate pyruvate survival during transfer and photocatalytic conversion at the upstream concentration in the presence of its coproducts.